/*
===========================================================================

dhewm3-rt Vulkan — gi_probe_resolve.comp — probe atlas -> screen.

Writes vkRT.giBuffer and vk_gi_probe.cpp repoints giReadView at it, so
gi_composite.frag keeps sampling one 2D image and knows nothing about probes.

  mode 0 — the real resolve (G2).  Reconstruct the pixel's world position and
           shading normal, offset along the normal and toward the eye, then take
           the 8 lattice probes surrounding that point: trilinear weight x a
           wrapped cosine term x Chebyshev visibility, normalised.  The
           irradiance fetch direction is the NORMAL; the probe direction only
           weights the blend, and the visibility lookup uses the direction from
           the probe BACK to the receiver.

  mode 1 — probe spheres tinted by the irradiance they have stored, in the
           direction the sphere is seen from.  Convergence readout for G1: a
           probe in a red-lit room must read red within ~16 frames.
  mode 2 — per-pixel probe weights (G3).  magenta = not one of the 8 probes had
           usable data, so GI is black here.  Otherwise a blue->red ramp on the
           FRACTION OF WEIGHT CHEBYSHEV REJECTED: blue = every probe is visible
           (open room), red = visibility is throwing nearly everything away
           (hugging a wall).  Availability of probe data is mode 4's job, so the
           two overlays compose rather than overlap.
  mode 3 — leak detector (G3), and the gate for this chunk.  Chebyshev is an
           ESTIMATE of visibility; this traces a ray query from the receiver to
           each probe for the GROUND TRUTH and colour-codes the disagreement:
             green = they agree (whatever the verdict)
             red   = ray says OCCLUDED but Chebyshev let it through
                     -> light leaking through geometry, a pillar-2 violation
             blue  = ray says VISIBLE but Chebyshev blocked it
                     -> over-darkening; lost energy, not a pillar violation
           Weighted by each probe's trilinear share, so the colour is "how much
           of this pixel's GI is wrong", not merely "something disagreed".
           At r_rtGIProbeVisibility 0 this shows the RAW leak surface with no
           mitigation, which is the before-picture to tune against; at 1 it
           shows the residual.  Costs up to 8 ray queries per pixel — an
           overlay, never the shipping path.
  mode 5 — distance atlas readout, owed from G1 (nothing had ever looked at the
           visibility moments and mode 3's Chebyshev is built on them).
           Spheres are tinted by the stored mean — already normalised to [0,1]
           by maxRayDist — on a blue->green->red ramp; magenta means
           mean2 < mean^2, algebraically impossible for a real second moment and
           therefore a broken map rather than a mistuned one.
  mode 6 — RAW backface fraction per probe, blue(0)->green(0.5)->red(1).
  mode 7 — RAW miss fraction, same ramp.
           Modes 4 and 6/7 answer different questions. Mode 4 shows the VERDICT,
           so a wrong verdict and a mistuned threshold look identical; 6/7 show
           the measurement the verdict was made from. An air probe SHOULD read
           blue in mode 6 (it sees front faces); if it reads green or red the
           statistic itself is wrong and no threshold will rescue it.
  mode 8 — G5b fast-bucket overlay, and it answers two questions at once:
           GREEN channel = the fraction of this pixel's resolved irradiance that
             came from the fast bucket, i.e. how much of the GI here is actually
             being factorized.  Black means the classifier found nothing to do
             here; a bright room under a flickering light should be well lit.
           RED channel = how many flickering lights reach this point, /3.
             Exactly ONE of them is bucketed (vk_gi.cpp picks the highest-
             importance one, because a bucket's single gain can only be exact
             for a single light); the others keep flickering correctly in
             direct light, volumetrics and reflections, but their probe GI is
             EMA-smeared as it was before G5b.  So red is the measure of what
             this chunk is still leaving on the table: red staying dark says
             one bucket covers the level, and red lighting up marks the places
             that would need a per-PROBE gain to fix.
           Counted by containment against the light SSBO, not by tracing — the
           question is which lights REACH the point, and a shadowed one would
           still have shared the gain.

Every mode ADDS onto the lit scene.  For the sphere overlays (1, 4, 5, 6, 7)
that is deliberate — the thing being checked is where the probes sit in the
world, and a black background makes that harder to judge than the room does.

For the per-pixel overlays (2, 3) it is a LIMITATION, not a choice:
VK_RT_CompositeGI has only an additive pipeline and the direct-light
interactions are drawn after it, so the scene tints the measurement.  Read hue
ratios, not absolute colour, and distrust these two modes on bright surfaces.
Fixing it needs a replace composite AND suppression of the later scene draws.

Descriptors (set 0):
  binding 0 — irradiance atlas (sampler2D)
  binding 1 — giBuffer rgba16f 2D (write)
  binding 2 — GIProbeParams UBO       (declared by gi_probe_common.glsl)
  binding 3 — probe state SSBO        (declared by gi_probe_common.glsl)
  binding 4 — distance atlas (sampler2D)
  binding 5 — depthSampler
  binding 6 — gbufNormalSampler
  binding 7 — TLAS (mode 3 only, but statically accessed, so always bound)
  binding 8 — probe stats SSBO (modes 6/7), shared with gi_probe_blend.comp

20260906_froxel_probe_gi.md Part B, chunks G0-G3.

This file is a new addition with dhewm3-rt.  It was created with the aid of GenAI,
and may reference the existing Dhewm3 OpenGL and vkDoom3 Vulkan updates of the Doom 3 GPL Source
Code.

It is distributed under the same modified GNU General Public License Version 3 of the original Doom 3 GPL Source
Code release.

===========================================================================
*/

#version 460
#extension GL_EXT_ray_query : require

layout(local_size_x = 8, local_size_y = 8, local_size_z = 1) in;

layout(set = 0, binding = 0) uniform sampler2D irradianceAtlas;
layout(set = 0, binding = 1, rgba16f) uniform image2D giOut;
layout(set = 0, binding = 4) uniform sampler2D distanceAtlas;
layout(set = 0, binding = 5) uniform sampler2D depthSampler;
layout(set = 0, binding = 6) uniform sampler2D gbufNormalSampler;
layout(set = 0, binding = 7) uniform accelerationStructureEXT tlas;

// Same declaration as gi_probe_blend.comp's binding 5, at a different binding
// because this pipeline has its own layout.  Read-only here: modes 6 and 7 show
// the RAW statistic, before the threshold and dead band turn it into a flag.
struct GIProbeStats {
    float backface;
    float miss;
};

layout(set = 0, binding = 8, std430) readonly buffer GIProbeStatsBuf {
    GIProbeStats probes[];
} probeStats;

// G5b mode 8 only — reads the containment fields and the FAST flag.
//
// rt_light_struct.glsl is struct-only and safe here; rt_light_eval.glsl is not,
// because it declares a shadow-ray payload and calls traceRayEXT, neither of
// which exists in a compute shader.
#include "rt_light_struct.glsl"

// Padding twin of RTLightCookie so the light array's stride matches the upload.
// Not rt_light_cookie.glsl's own struct: that file's samplers need set=1's
// bindless matTextures[], which this shader does not bind.
struct GIResolveCookie {
    vec4 planeS;
    vec4 planeT;
    vec4 planeQ;
    uint imageIndex;
    uint pad0; uint pad1; uint pad2;
};

layout(set = 0, binding = 9, std430) readonly buffer GIResolveLightBuf {
    int   numLights;
    float bounceScale;
    float giRadius;
    float emissiveScale;
    float reflAmbientScale;
    int   falloffMode;   // unused here (no attenuation), mirrored for layout parity
    float falloffReach;  // used by the mode-8 containment test so the overlay matches
    float _pad0[1];
    RTLight lights[RT_LIGHT_MAX_LIGHTS];
    GIResolveCookie cookies[RT_LIGHT_MAX_LIGHTS];
} giLights;

#define GIPROBE_SET 0
#include "gi_probe_common.glsl"
// Satisfies rt_indirect.glsl's includer contract (a sampler2D named
// depthSampler, declared above).
#include "rt_indirect.glsl"
#include "rt_gbuf_normal.glsl"

// Floor under the Chebyshev factor. A probe is never weighted to exactly zero:
// at zero the interpolation goes discontinuous across the wall and the seam is
// more objectionable than the residual leak. DDGI uses the same 0.05.
#define GIPROBE_VIS_FLOOR 0.05

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Ray vs the probe lattice (sphere overlays only).
//
// Stepping the ray and testing the NEAREST lattice point at each step, rather
// than iterating the whole grid: at spacing/2 steps no sphere of radius <
// spacing/2 can be stepped over, and each test is an analytic ray-sphere so the
// result is exact rather than a marched approximation.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// tRange   — how far to march (bounds cost).
// tVisible — distance to the depth-buffer surface along this ray.
// xrayMask — probe flags that are allowed to draw THROUGH geometry.
//
// The drawability test lives inside the loop on purpose. Returning the nearest
// sphere of any class and rejecting it afterwards silently hides every probe
// behind it: an ordinary probe one pixel past the wall would suppress the buried
// probe the overlay exists to show. This returns the nearest DRAWABLE sphere.
bool gip_TraceProbeSphere(vec3 ro, vec3 rd, float tRange, float tVisible, uint xrayMask, float radius,
                          out ivec3 hitCell, out vec3 hitNormal, out float hitT)
{
    hitCell = ivec3(0);
    hitNormal = vec3(0.0);
    hitT = tRange;

    float spacing = max(gp.gridOrigin.w, 1.0);
    float step = spacing * 0.5;
    float r2 = radius * radius;
    float best = tRange;
    bool found = false;

    // 256 steps * spacing/2 covers 8192 units at the 64-unit default, well past
    // any Doom 3 room; the loop exits on tRange long before that indoors.
    for (int i = 0; i < 256; i++)
    {
        float t = float(i) * step;
        if (t > tRange)
            break;

        vec3 p = ro + rd * t;
        ivec3 cell = ivec3(round(p / spacing));
        if (!gip_CellInGrid(cell))
            continue;

        int idx = gip_StorageIndex(cell);
        vec3 c = gip_ProbePosAt(cell, idx);
        vec3 oc = c - ro;
        float tca = dot(oc, rd);
        float d2 = dot(oc, oc) - tca * tca;
        if (d2 > r2)
            continue;

        float t0 = tca - sqrt(max(0.0, r2 - d2));
        if (t0 < 0.0 || t0 >= best)
            continue;

        if (t0 > tVisible && (probeState.probes[idx].flags & xrayMask) == 0u)
            continue; // behind the world and not x-ray class

        best = t0;
        hitCell = cell;
        hitNormal = normalize((ro + rd * t0) - c);
        hitT = t0;
        found = true;
    }

    return found;
}

// Blue -> cyan -> green -> yellow -> red over [0,1].  Overlay-only.
vec3 gip_Ramp(float t)
{
    t = clamp(t, 0.0, 1.0);
    return clamp(vec3(t * 3.0 - 1.5, 1.5 - abs(t * 3.0 - 1.5), 1.5 - t * 3.0), vec3(0.0), vec3(1.0));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One of the 8 surrounding probes, with everything both the resolve and the
// overlays need.  Factored out so mode 3 can reuse the exact weights mode 0
// computes — an overlay that recomputes them differently tests nothing.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
struct GIProbeTap
{
    int   idx;
    vec3  pos;
    float weight; // trilinear x wrapped cosine x crush, BEFORE visibility
    float cheb;   // Chebyshev factor, 1.0 when r_rtGIProbeVisibility is 0
    bool  valid;
};

GIProbeTap gip_Tap(int i, vec3 worldPos, vec3 normal, vec3 samplePos, ivec3 baseCell, vec3 frac)
{
    GIProbeTap t;
    t.idx = 0;
    t.pos = worldPos;
    t.weight = 0.0;
    t.cheb = 1.0;
    t.valid = false;

    ivec3 off = ivec3(i & 1, (i >> 1) & 1, (i >> 2) & 1);
    ivec3 cell = baseCell + off;
    if (!gip_CellInGrid(cell))
        return t;

    t.idx = gip_StorageIndex(cell);
    uint flags = probeState.probes[t.idx].flags;
    // An untraced probe's atlas tile still belongs to whichever probe last
    // occupied it (storage is toroidal), so it is not "black", it is another
    // room — exclude it outright.
    if ((flags & GIPROBE_FLAG_TRACED) == 0u)
        return t;
    // Buried in a brush, or adrift in the void outside the level hull. Either
    // way its irradiance describes somewhere the receiver cannot see and its
    // distance moments cannot be reasoned about, so it gets no weight at all.
    if ((flags & GIPROBE_FLAG_UNUSABLE) != 0u)
        return t;

    vec3 tri = mix(vec3(1.0) - frac, frac, vec3(off));
    float w = tri.x * tri.y * tri.z;
    if (w <= 0.0)
        return t;

    t.pos = gip_ProbePosAt(cell, t.idx);

    // Wrapped cosine against the true (unbiased) direction to the probe: a
    // probe behind the surface contributes almost nothing, but never exactly
    // nothing, or the interpolation goes discontinuous at grazing angles and
    // shows as banding.
    vec3 toProbe = t.pos - worldPos;
    float lenSq = dot(toProbe, toProbe);
    vec3 dirToProbe = (lenSq > 1e-8) ? (toProbe * inversesqrt(lenSq)) : normal;
    float wrapped = max(1e-4, (dot(dirToProbe, normal) + 1.0) * 0.5);
    w *= wrapped * wrapped + 0.2;

    // Crush the tail so a probe contributing a sliver does not drag a
    // neighbouring room's colour in at full trilinear strength.
    const float crush = 0.2;
    if (w < crush)
        w *= (w * w) / (crush * crush);

    t.weight = w;
    t.valid = true;

    // Chebyshev, from the BIASED position — the same point the trilinear
    // fraction was taken at, and offset off the surface so the receiver's own
    // wall does not read as its own occluder.
    if (gp.misc.z != 0)
    {
        vec3 probeToPoint = samplePos - t.pos;
        float dist = length(probeToPoint);
        if (dist > 1e-6)
        {
            float invD = 1.0 / max(gp.tune.w, 1.0);
            vec2 m = texture(distanceAtlas, gip_AtlasUV(t.idx, probeToPoint / dist, gp.atlas.w)).rg;
            t.cheb = gip_Chebyshev(m, dist * invD);
        }
    }

    return t;
}

// True when geometry stands between the receiver and the probe.  Ground truth
// for mode 3 — this is what Chebyshev is approximating.
//
// OPAQUE GEOMETRY ONLY, and that is the whole point.  The moments this is
// checking were built by probe rays that went through gi_ray.rahit, which
// DISCARDS alpha-tested geometry below its threshold — so grating holes and
// decals do not occlude a probe ray.  Forcing gl_RayFlagsOpaqueEXT here instead
// made every perforated surface read solid, and since Mars City's floors are
// grating, the overlay reported a screen of leaks that were Chebyshev being
// right.  No confirm call and no opaque flag means non-opaque candidates are
// simply never committed and only opaque geometry occludes.
//
// Residual mismatch, deliberate: rahit ACCEPTS non-opaque geometry that is not
// alpha-tested (glass), so glass occludes a probe ray but reads as visible
// here.  That direction turns a real leak into a green or blue pixel rather
// than inventing a red one — for a leak detector, under-reporting on rare
// geometry beats crying wolf on every floor.  A faithful test would need the
// material table and UV interpolation in this pass, which is not worth it for
// an overlay.
bool gip_ProbeOccluded(vec3 from, vec3 to)
{
    vec3 d = to - from;
    float len = length(d);
    // Below the bias span there is no interval left to test, and tMin > tMax
    // would make the query meaningless rather than merely empty.
    if (len < 0.05)
        return false;

    rayQueryEXT rq;
    rayQueryInitializeEXT(rq, tlas, gl_RayFlagsTerminateOnFirstHitEXT,
                          0xFEu, // skip the player body/hands, same mask the vol passes use
                          from, 0.01, d / len, len - 0.01);

    while (rayQueryProceedEXT(rq))
    {
    }

    return rayQueryGetIntersectionTypeEXT(rq, true) != gl_RayQueryCommittedIntersectionNoneEXT;
}

// Shared setup for every per-pixel mode: world position, shading normal, and the
// biased sample point the lattice is addressed at.
struct GIProbeSite
{
    vec3  worldPos;
    vec3  normal;
    vec3  samplePos;
    ivec3 baseCell;
    vec3  frac;
    bool  valid; // false for sky / pixels the prepass never wrote
};

GIProbeSite gip_Site(ivec2 coord, ivec2 fullSize)
{
    GIProbeSite s;
    s.worldPos = vec3(0.0);
    s.normal = vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
    s.samplePos = vec3(0.0);
    s.baseCell = ivec3(0);
    s.frac = vec3(0.0);
    s.valid = false;

    float depth = texelFetch(depthSampler, coord, 0).r;
    if (depth >= 1.0)
        return s; // sky gets no GI, same as gi_ray.rgen

    s.worldPos = rt_ReconstructWorldPos(coord, fullSize, gp.invViewProj);
    vec3 toEye = gp.cameraPosW.xyz - s.worldPos;
    vec3 viewDir = (dot(toEye, toEye) > 1e-8) ? normalize(toEye) : vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0);

    if (!rt_GbufNormalAt(coord, gp.misc.y, viewDir, s.normal))
        s.normal = rt_ReconstructNormal(coord, fullSize, gp.invViewProj, s.worldPos, viewDir);

    // Normal bias lifts the point off its own surface; view bias pushes it
    // toward the eye, which is what stops a grazing wall from occluding itself
    // in the Chebyshev test. DDGI carries both for the same reason.
    s.samplePos = s.worldPos + s.normal * max(gp.tune.y, 0.0) + viewDir * max(gp.tune2.y, 0.0);

    vec3 g = s.samplePos / max(gp.gridOrigin.w, 1.0);
    s.baseCell = ivec3(floor(g));
    s.frac = clamp(g - vec3(s.baseCell), vec3(0.0), vec3(1.0));
    s.valid = true;
    return s;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The G2/G3 resolve: irradiance at the site from its 8 surrounding probes.
// wBefore/wAfter report the weight Chebyshev removed, for mode 2.  Returns
// false when not one probe had usable data — the caller must leave GI at zero
// rather than invent a value, and mode 4's red spheres locate that case.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// G5b: fastOut reports the fast buckets' share of the same sum, already scaled
// by its gain, so mode 8 can show what fraction of the GI here is factorized
// without resampling. irradiance is the TOTAL (stable + fast), i.e. what the
// shipping path wants.
bool gip_SampleIrradiance(GIProbeSite s, out vec3 irradiance, out vec3 fastOut, out float wBefore, out float wAfter)
{
    irradiance = vec3(0.0);
    fastOut = vec3(0.0);
    wBefore = 0.0;
    wAfter = 0.0;

    // All eight fetches are in the receiver's normal direction; only the tile
    // differs, so the octahedral encode is hoisted out of the loop.
    vec2 oct = gip_OctEncode(normalize(s.normal)) * 0.5 + 0.5;

    // G5b: the buckets share the Chebyshev weights — they describe the same
    // geometry from the same probe, and only differ in which lights' transport
    // was cached. Each extra bucket therefore costs 8 more bilinear taps and no
    // extra weighting work. Those taps are the whole argument against K = 2:
    // G2 measured this loop issue-rate bound on its scattered fetches.
    int buckets = gip_IrrBuckets();

    vec3 sum = vec3(0.0);
    vec3 sumFast = vec3(0.0);

    for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++)
    {
        GIProbeTap t = gip_Tap(i, s.worldPos, s.normal, s.samplePos, s.baseCell, s.frac);
        if (!t.valid)
            continue;

        wBefore += t.weight;
        float w = t.weight * max(t.cheb, GIPROBE_VIS_FLOOR);
        wAfter += w;

        sum += texture(irradianceAtlas, gip_IrrAtlasUVOct(t.idx, 0, oct, gp.atlas.z)).rgb * w;

        for (int k = 1; k < buckets; k++)
            sumFast += texture(irradianceAtlas, gip_IrrAtlasUVOct(t.idx, k, oct, gp.atlas.z)).rgb *
                       (w * gip_BucketGain(k));
    }

    if (wAfter <= 1e-6)
        return false;

    float invW = 1.0 / wAfter;
    fastOut = sumFast * invW;
    irradiance = sum * invW + fastOut;
    return true;
}

void main()
{
    ivec2 local = ivec2(gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy);
    ivec2 rectOff = gp.rect.xy;
    ivec2 rectExt = gp.rect.zw;
    if (local.x >= rectExt.x || local.y >= rectExt.y)
        return;

    ivec2 coord = local + rectOff;
    ivec2 outSize = gp.screen.zw;
    if (coord.x >= outSize.x || coord.y >= outSize.y)
        return;

    int mode = gp.misc.x;
    ivec2 fullSize = gp.screen.xy;
    ivec2 fullCoord = min(coord, fullSize - ivec2(1));

    vec3 outColour = vec3(0.0);

    if (mode == 0)
    {
        GIProbeSite s = gip_Site(fullCoord, fullSize);
        vec3 irradiance, fastPart;
        float wBefore, wAfter;
        if (s.valid && gip_SampleIrradiance(s, irradiance, fastPart, wBefore, wAfter))
        {
            outColour = irradiance * gp.tune.z;

            // Same energy-neutral contrast push gi_ray.rgen applies, so an
            // r_rtGIProbes A/B at matched r_rtGIStrength/r_rtGIContrast
            // compares the sampling structure and nothing else.
            if (gp.tune2.x > 0.0)
            {
                vec3 grayRef = vec3(dot(outColour, vec3(1.0 / 3.0)));
                outColour = max(grayRef + (outColour - grayRef) * (1.0 + gp.tune2.x), vec3(0.0));
            }
        }
    }
    else if (mode == 2)
    {
        GIProbeSite s = gip_Site(fullCoord, fullSize);
        if (s.valid)
        {
            vec3 irradiance, fastPart;
            float wBefore, wAfter;
            if (!gip_SampleIrradiance(s, irradiance, fastPart, wBefore, wAfter) || wBefore <= 1e-6)
                outColour = vec3(1.0, 0.0, 1.0); // no usable probe — GI is black here
            else
                outColour = gip_Ramp(1.0 - wAfter / wBefore);
        }
    }
    else if (mode == 3)
    {
        GIProbeSite s = gip_Site(fullCoord, fullSize);
        if (s.valid)
        {
            float wTotal = 0.0;
            float wLeak = 0.0; // occluded, but Chebyshev let it through
            float wDark = 0.0; // visible, but Chebyshev blocked it

            for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++)
            {
                GIProbeTap t = gip_Tap(i, s.worldPos, s.normal, s.samplePos, s.baseCell, s.frac);
                if (!t.valid)
                    continue;

                wTotal += t.weight;

                // Trace from the biased point, matching what Chebyshev was
                // asked about — testing a different ray would make any
                // disagreement meaningless.
                bool occluded = gip_ProbeOccluded(s.samplePos, t.pos);
                bool passed = t.cheb > 0.5;

                if (occluded && passed)
                    wLeak += t.weight;
                else if (!occluded && !passed)
                    wDark += t.weight;
            }

            if (wTotal > 1e-6)
            {
                float leak = wLeak / wTotal;
                float dark = wDark / wTotal;
                outColour = vec3(leak, max(0.0, 1.0 - leak - dark), dark);
            }
            else
            {
                // MAGENTA: all 8 neighbours excluded (G4) or zero-weighted, so
                // this surface gets no probe GI at all. Leaving outColour alone
                // here would render the scene unmodified and read as "fine" —
                // the total absence of GI is the worst case, not the best.
                outColour = vec3(1.0, 0.0, 1.0);
            }
        }
    }
    else if (mode == 8)
    {
        // G5b. Green = how much of this pixel's GI is coming from the fast
        // bucket; red = how many distinct fast lights reach the point, /3.
        //
        // The two are read together. Green bright and red dark is the healthy
        // case and says K = 1 is exact here. Green bright and red bright says two
        // or more fast lights share one gain at this point, so their individual
        // flicker phases are being averaged — the only condition that argues for
        // K = 2, and the reason this overlay exists before any tuning.
        GIProbeSite s = gip_Site(fullCoord, fullSize);
        if (s.valid)
        {
            vec3 irradiance, fastPart;
            float wBefore, wAfter;
            if (gip_SampleIrradiance(s, irradiance, fastPart, wBefore, wAfter))
            {
                float lumTotal = dot(irradiance, vec3(1.0 / 3.0));
                float lumFast = dot(fastPart, vec3(1.0 / 3.0));
                float frac = (lumTotal > 1e-6) ? clamp(lumFast / lumTotal, 0.0, 1.0) : 0.0;

                // Containment only, no shadow ray: the question is which fast
                // lights REACH this point and therefore share the bucket's one
                // gain. A shadowed light still shares it.
                int fastCount = 0;
                int n = min(giLights.numLights, RT_LIGHT_MAX_LIGHTS);
                for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
                {
                    if ((giLights.lights[i].flags & GI_LIGHT_FLAG_FAST) == 0u)
                        continue;

                    vec3 lPos = giLights.lights[i].posRadius.xyz;
                    vec4 bx = giLights.lights[i].boxExtents;
                    vec3 toPoint = s.worldPos - lPos;

                    if (giLights.lights[i].lightType == 0u)
                    {
                        vec3 nl = abs(toPoint) / max(bx.xyz, vec3(0.001));
                        if (max(max(nl.x, nl.y), nl.z) < giLights.falloffReach)
                            fastCount++;
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        vec4 cd = giLights.lights[i].coneDir;
                        float along = dot(toPoint, cd.xyz);
                        if (along > 0.0 && along <= bx.w &&
                            along / max(length(toPoint), 0.001) >= cd.w)
                            fastCount++;
                    }
                }

                outColour = vec3(float(fastCount) / 3.0, frac, 0.0);
            }
            else
            {
                outColour = vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0); // no usable probe — same case mode 2 paints magenta
            }
        }
    }
    else if (mode == 1 || mode == 4 || mode == 5 || mode == 6 || mode == 7)
    {
        vec2 uv = (vec2(fullCoord) + 0.5) / vec2(fullSize);
        vec3 ro = gp.cameraPosW.xyz;
        vec3 rd = gip_RayDirForUV(uv);

        // Spheres are occluded by the world: a probe behind a wall must not show
        // through it, or the overlay reads as "probes everywhere" and says
        // nothing about placement.
        float depth = texelFetch(depthSampler, fullCoord, 0).r;
        float tSurface = 1.0e6;
        if (depth < 1.0)
            tSurface = length(rt_ReconstructWorldPos(fullCoord, fullSize, gp.invViewProj) - ro);

        // Mode 4 marches PAST the surface, because the probes it most needs to
        // show are the unusable ones — depth-occluding a probe buried in a wall
        // makes it invisible by construction, which is exactly the state G4
        // classifies. The trace itself enforces drawability, so an ordinary
        // probe behind the world cannot hide a buried one further along.
        // Modes 6/7 read the raw statistic and are diagnosing exactly the probes
        // the flags got wrong, so they x-ray on the same terms as mode 4.
        const uint xray = GIPROBE_FLAG_UNUSABLE;
        bool wide = (mode == 4 || mode == 6 || mode == 7);
        float tRange = wide ? 1.0e6 : tSurface;
        uint mask = wide ? xray : 0u;

        ivec3 cell;
        vec3 n;
        float tHit;
        if (gip_TraceProbeSphere(ro, rd, tRange, tSurface, mask, max(gp.debug.y, 0.25), cell, n, tHit))
        {
            int idx = gip_StorageIndex(cell);
            uint flags = probeState.probes[idx].flags;

            if (mode == 4)
            {
                if ((flags & GIPROBE_FLAG_OUTSIDE) != 0u)
                    outColour = vec3(1.0, 0.8, 0.0); // void, outside the level hull
                else if ((flags & GIPROBE_FLAG_INSIDE) != 0u)
                    outColour = vec3(0.0, 0.3, 1.0); // buried in a solid brush
                else if ((flags & GIPROBE_FLAG_TRACED) != 0u)
                    outColour = vec3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0); // usable
                else
                    outColour = vec3(1.0, 0.0, 0.0); // never traced

                // Cheap lambert against the view so the spheres read as spheres
                // and a lattice is legible as a lattice.
                outColour *= mix(0.35, 1.0, max(0.0, dot(n, -rd)));

                // Halved when x-rayed through geometry, so "buried in that wall"
                // stays distinguishable from "sitting in front of it" — without
                // this a flagged sphere gives no depth cue at all, and a
                // misclassified probe in open air reads identically to a
                // correctly classified one inside the wall behind it.
                if (tHit > tSurface)
                    outColour *= 0.5;
            }
            else if (mode == 6 || mode == 7)
            {
                if ((flags & GIPROBE_FLAG_TRACED) == 0u)
                {
                    outColour = vec3(0.4, 0.0, 0.4); // never traced, nothing measured
                }
                else
                {
                    // The RAW statistic on the ramp, with no threshold applied.
                    // Mode 4 can only say "flagged"; this says by how much, which
                    // is the difference between a mistuned threshold and a
                    // statistic that is measuring the wrong thing. The ramp is
                    // blue(0) -> green(0.5) -> red(1), so an air probe reading
                    // near 0 is blue and one reading near 1 is red.
                    float v = (mode == 6) ? probeStats.probes[idx].backface : probeStats.probes[idx].miss;
                    outColour = gip_Ramp(clamp(v, 0.0, 1.0));
                }
                outColour *= mix(0.35, 1.0, max(0.0, dot(n, -rd)));
                if (tHit > tSurface)
                    outColour *= 0.5;
            }
            else if (mode == 5)
            {
                if ((flags & GIPROBE_FLAG_TRACED) == 0u)
                {
                    outColour = vec3(0.4, 0.0, 0.4);
                }
                else
                {
                    // Both moments are already normalised by maxRayDist — see
                    // gi_probe_blend.comp's header for why they have to be.
                    vec2 m = texture(distanceAtlas, gip_AtlasUV(idx, n, gp.atlas.w)).rg;
                    // Negative variance cannot happen for a real second moment;
                    // if it does, the lobe weights or the EMA are wrong, not
                    // merely mistuned.  Catches an fp16 overflow as NaN too.
                    if (!(m.y >= m.x * m.x - 1e-3))
                        outColour = vec3(1.0, 0.0, 1.0);
                    else
                        outColour = gip_Ramp(m.x);
                    outColour *= mix(0.35, 1.0, max(0.0, dot(n, -rd)));
                }
            }
            else
            {
                // Bucket 0 — the stable set. Mode 1 is "what has this probe
                // stored", and adding a fast bucket scaled by a gain that moves
                // every frame would make a steady overlay strobe.
                vec2 auv = gip_IrrAtlasUV(idx, 0, n, gp.atlas.z);
                outColour = texture(irradianceAtlas, auv).rgb * gp.debug.x;

                // An untraced probe has nothing to show; magenta says "no data"
                // rather than letting a black tile read as "this room is dark".
                if ((flags & GIPROBE_FLAG_TRACED) == 0u)
                    outColour = vec3(0.4, 0.0, 0.4);
            }
        }
    }

    imageStore(giOut, coord, vec4(max(outColour, vec3(0.0)), 1.0));
}
